(occurring daily in hundreds of cases.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(    Miss Jane Smith is a lady who has been) Tj T*
(brought up without the slightest instruction in) Tj T*
(business matters, indeed has rather plumed her-) Tj T*
(self on the idea of being quite above such things.) Tj T*
(Suddenly she finds herself dependent upon others) Tj T*
(for guidance and advice.  She would like to act) Tj T*
(for herself if she only knew how to do so safely,) Tj T*
(being of a somewhat suspicious temperament and) Tj T*
(mistrustful of advice from friends or acquaint-) Tj T*
(ances.  Even the highly respectable lawyer,) Tj T*
(who has handed her a packet of documents) Tj T*
(and г500 in cash \(a legacy from her uncle\), with) Tj T*
(much sage counsel, she is not quite sure about,) Tj T*
(for she has imbibed the idea from her youth that) Tj T*
(lawyers are not always to be trusted.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(    The packet of documents in the tin box as) Tj T*
(they came to her is set aside in a safe place for) Tj T*
(the moment, but the bank-notes and gold are a) Tj T*
(matter of serious concern to her.  She fears to) Tj T*
(carry them about her person lest she should) Tj T*
(lose them, or be robbed, and feels sure that if) Tj T*
(kept in the house they will attract any burglars) Tj T*
(that may be in the neighbourhood.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
